{
  /* ==============================================================================================
   * slatwall-ts — the EMIT / PACKAGING program for the extracted Slatwall Catalog slice.
   *
   * This file has no legacy counterpart. The Slatwall repository contains zero Node or TypeScript
   * artifacts (AAP 0.3.1), so nothing here is carried over from the CFML tree, and nothing outside
   * slatwall-ts/ is ever part of a program defined here: every path below is subtree-relative and
   * none of them walks up out of this directory. That is how the additive-only invariant of
   * AAP 0.4.1.1 (transformation rule TR-6, guideline 0.8.2/5) is enforced mechanically rather than
   * by convention — the legacy tree stays byte-for-byte unchanged, including by not being compiled.
   *
   * Its whole requirement is one sentence, AAP 0.4.1.2: "Emit configuration for the bundle step,
   * excluding tests."
   *
   * WHY IT EXISTS — the division of labour with the sibling tsconfig.json:
   *
   *   tsconfig.json        Type-checks src/** AND test/** AND jest.config.ts, and never emits
   *                        (noEmit true). `npm run typecheck` loads it, and typescript-eslint's
   *                        projectService resolves its file list, so production source and the
   *                        test suite are held to ONE identical rule set instead of the tests
   *                        getting a relaxed configuration of their own.
   *
   *   tsconfig.build.json  This file. The same rules over a narrower program: shippable src/**
   *                        only, with emit switched on. Nothing test-only can reach a Lambda
   *                        artifact for the simplest possible reason — nothing test-only is in
   *                        the program. The 20 test files of AAP 0.4.1.12 and the Jest
   *                        configuration are development artifacts and stop here.
   *
   * Program scope and emit are the ONLY differences between the two files, and that is deliberate.
   *
   * WHAT CONSUMES IT: `tsc -p tsconfig.build.json`. The bundler does not read this file —
   * build/esbuild.mjs derives its inputs from the src/handlers entry points plus the import graph,
   * and applies its own --platform=node --target=node20 --format=cjs. So the two steps divide the
   * work: this program proves the shippable set type-checks AND emits cleanly under the same
   * strict contract as the gate that approved it, then the bundler packages it. Run together they
   * are the deployability bar of AAP 0.8.3.10 — `npm run typecheck && npm run build` — which is
   * the whole acceptance criterion, since infrastructure as code is out of scope and no runtime
   * declaration, function definition or deployment call is authored anywhere in this deliverable
   * (AAP 0.5.5).
   * ============================================================================================ */
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    /* --- INHERITED, AND NEVER WEAKENED -------------------------------------------------------
     * Everything about the language and the type system comes from ./tsconfig.json and is
     * deliberately ABSENT below. This file does not restate — and must never override — strict,
     * exactOptionalPropertyTypes or noUncheckedIndexedAccess, which are the three flags that
     * constitute AAP 0.7.3 standard 1 ("strict type safety"); nor target ES2022 / lib ES2022 /
     * module NodeNext / moduleResolution NodeNext; nor noImplicitOverride, noImplicitReturns,
     * noFallthroughCasesInSwitch, useUnknownInCatchVariables, isolatedModules, esModuleInterop,
     * forceConsistentCasingInFileNames or skipLibCheck.
     *
     * Inherit and narrow; never duplicate. Duplicating the compiler options would let the two
     * files drift, and a drifted build config is worse than no build config at all: it would ship
     * an artifact checked LESS strictly than the gate which approved it, which is precisely the
     * false assurance this port exists to avoid. The prohibition has no exceptions. No relaxing
     * option may ever be added here — not a falsified strict flag, not a disabled strictNullChecks,
     * not an admitted implicit any, not a suppression flag, and not skipLibCheck pressed into
     * service to hide a real error. If a file under src/ fails to compile, THE SOURCE IS FIXED.
     * `tsc --showConfig -p tsconfig.build.json` prints the resolved set and is the way to prove
     * the inheritance is intact rather than assume it.
     *
     * NO MODULE PATH ALIASES ARE DECLARED HERE, and none exist in the base either: neither the
     * `baseUrl` anchor nor the `paths` map appears in either file (AAP 0.4.3.5). Every
     * intra-subtree import is a relative path. This is a correctness requirement, not a style
     * preference — esbuild does not honour tsconfig alias maps the way tsc does, so an alias that
     * type-checks cleanly here could fail to resolve at bundle time and the packaged handler would
     * throw MODULE_NOT_FOUND on cold start, in the one place there is no compiler left to catch
     * it. Identical resolution across tsc, ts-jest and esbuild also keeps the hexagonal boundary
     * legible (AAP 0.7.3 standard 4): a relative specifier makes a domain -> adapter or
     * service -> handler direction violation visible in the import line itself.
     *
     * The five options below are therefore the entire content of this file — two overrides that
     * turn a type-check program into an emit program, and three emit-only settings restated at
     * their inherited values because this is the only program that actually emits. Nothing else is
     * added: per AAP 0.7.3 standard 9 no option appears that cannot be justified in a line, which
     * is why declarationMap, composite, incremental, tsBuildInfoFile, removeComments and the
     * decorator-metadata family are all absent, and why they should stay absent. */

    /* OVERRIDE 1 of 2 — emit. The base sets noEmit true so that `npm run typecheck`, and any bare
     * `tsc -p tsconfig.json`, can never scatter JavaScript into dist/. This is the one
     * configuration that flips it, which is exactly what makes the base's setting meaningful. */
    "noEmit": false,

    /* OVERRIDE 2 of 2 — emit layout. The base uses rootDir "." because its program spans src/,
     * test/ and jest.config.ts. Here the program is src/ alone, so rootDir "src" makes dist/
     * mirror src/ directly (dist/handlers/productHandler.js, not dist/src/handlers/...). That is
     * also the layout build/esbuild.mjs produces, since it sets outbase to src/, so a path in a
     * stack trace means the same thing whichever step produced the JavaScript. */
    "rootDir": "src",

    /* Restated at the inherited value; never divergent. outDir is an emit concern and this is the
     * emitting program, so the destination is pinned where emission is switched on rather than
     * left to be inferred from elsewhere. Three artifacts must agree on this one name, and do:
     * the `dist/` entry in slatwall-ts/.gitignore (build output is never committed), this option,
     * and the outDir in build/esbuild.mjs. Renaming it in one place alone would silently break
     * the other two. */
    "outDir": "dist",

    /* The one emit extra worth having: source maps are what let a stack trace from a deployed
     * invocation point back at the TypeScript that produced it. build/esbuild.mjs sets
     * sourcemap: true for the same reason, so both routes to an artifact stay debuggable. */
    "sourceMap": true,

    /* No .d.ts output. package.json declares "private": true and the deliverable is bundled
     * Lambda artifacts, so nothing outside this subtree ever consumes it as a typed library and
     * declaration files would be unread build products. declarationMap is absent for the same
     * reason (and TypeScript rejects it without declaration anyway). */
    "declaration": false
  },

  /* Program scope — the defining purpose of this file, per AAP 0.4.1.2 ("excluding tests").
   *
   * `include` admits shippable TypeScript under src/ and nothing else. `exclude` then states the
   * same boundary from the other side, belt-and-braces, so the intent survives any later edit to
   * `include`: the whole test/ tree, the recursive *.test.ts and *.spec.ts globs, and
   * jest.config.ts (a development configuration, not shippable code) are all kept out, alongside
   * the usual node_modules, dist and coverage directories.
   *
   * Two entries are absent on purpose. build/esbuild.mjs is .mjs rather than TypeScript, so it
   * belongs to no TypeScript program and needs no exclusion. And no pattern here walks up out of
   * this directory — every one is subtree-relative — because the CFML tree is reference-only.
   *
   * A note for anyone auditing what actually ships. `exclude` filters the initial file glob; it
   * cannot remove a file that an included file imports. Keeping test-only code out of the emitted
   * output therefore also depends on nothing under src/ importing from test/, which is a rule the
   * hexagonal layering already implies: test doubles live in test/support and are injected into
   * production classes through their constructors (AAP 0.4.3.1, 0.4.3.6), never imported by them.
   * The same reachability argument governs what the bundler pulls in. The AWS SDK is always marked
   * external in build/esbuild.mjs, because the Node 20 Lambda runtime already provides it and
   * bundling it would inflate the artifact for no gain. mysql2 — the single runtime dependency, and
   * the only package that ships inside the bundle (AAP 0.5.2.1) — is bundled by default, with
   * ESBUILD_EXTERNAL=mysql2 available to externalise it as well when the driver is supplied
   * alongside the artifact, which is the form the build proof of AAP 0.1.2.3 used.
   *
   * Finally, the version coupling worth knowing about when this service is eventually moved to a
   * newer runtime line. AAP 0.5.5 counts exactly four version-coupled artifacts in the whole
   * deliverable: --target=node20 in build/esbuild.mjs, the `engines` field together with .nvmrc,
   * the @types/node pin held on the 20.x line, and the version statements in README.md. This file
   * is deliberately not a fifth: it names no runtime and no Node version, and it inherits target
   * ES2022 from the base rather than restating it, so a runtime move touches those four artifacts
   * and leaves src/domain, src/services, src/ports and src/adapters untouched. */
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": [
    "node_modules",
    "dist",
    "coverage",
    "test",
    "**/*.test.ts",
    "**/*.spec.ts",
    "jest.config.ts"
  ]
}
